{"type":"assistant","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The issue was in `is_expired` in `repo/cache.py`. Expiry used strict `age > ttl`, so an entry whose age exactly matched the TTL was still treated as fresh and could live one time unit past when it should expire.\n\nThe docstring already defined the rule: expire when age reaches the TTL (`age == ttl` counts as expired). That’s now implemented as `age >= ttl`.\n\n`cache_summary` in `repo/rows.py` calls `is_expired`, so batch expiry counts align with that behavior. All tests pass."}]},"session_id":"07dd399b-d695-40ac-a0bd-bedb51b629c5"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","duration_ms":19052,"duration_api_ms":19052,"is_error":false,"result":"Exploring the codebase for cache configuration and TTL behavior.\n\n\nIdentifying the bug: `is_expired` uses `age > ttl`, so entries at exactly the TTL are treated as fresh. The docstring and tests expect `age >= ttl`.\n\n\nThe issue was in `is_expired` in `repo/cache.py`. Expiry used strict `age > ttl`, so an entry whose age exactly matched the TTL was still treated as fresh and could live one time unit past when it should expire.\n\nThe docstring already defined the rule: expire when age reaches the TTL (`age == ttl` counts as expired). That’s now implemented as `age >= ttl`.\n\n`cache_summary` in `repo/rows.py` calls `is_expired`, so batch expiry counts align with that behavior. All tests pass.","session_id":"07dd399b-d695-40ac-a0bd-bedb51b629c5","request_id":"958abb73-d74e-440f-bfee-6a413b163b84","usage":{"inputTokens":11533,"outputTokens":865,"cacheReadTokens":46716,"cacheWriteTokens":0}}
